Do not use ELF-only linker flags on Windows

LXQtCompilerSettings.cmake adds -Wl,-Bsymbolic-functions to the shared, module
and executable linker flags of every project that includes it. The block clears
the flag for AppleClang with the comment

    # -Bsymbolic* make sense for ELF only

and then falls through to setting it for everything else, Windows included.
PE/COFF has no symbol interposition for -Bsymbolic-functions to defeat, so the
flag is meaningless there in the first place.

It goes unnoticed under MINGW64/UCRT64 because GNU ld accepts the option and
ignores it. In CLANG64 the linker is lld, which refuses what it does not
recognise, and every LXQt library fails at the link step:

    lld: error: unknown argument: -Bsymbolic-functions
    clang++: error: linker command failed with exit code 1

Give Windows its own branch: --no-undefined is still wanted, since a Windows DLL
genuinely cannot have undefined symbols, but the symbolic flags are dropped.
Keeping it separate from the AppleClang branch rather than folding the two
together, because that one also swaps --no-undefined for -undefined,error.

Upstream: not submitted
Applies-to: all environments (only the WIN32 branch is new; ELF builds are
            unchanged)

--- a/cmake/modules/LXQtCompilerSettings.cmake
+++ b/cmake/modules/LXQtCompilerSettings.cmake
@@ -160,6 +160,14 @@
         set(NO_UNDEFINED_FLAGS "-Wl,-undefined,error")
         # -Bsymbolic* make sense for ELF only
         set(SYMBOLIC_FLAGS "")
+    elseif (WIN32)
+        set(NO_UNDEFINED_FLAGS "-Wl,--no-undefined")
+        # -Bsymbolic* make sense for ELF only, as the comment above says. PE/COFF
+        # has no symbol interposition to defeat, so the flag buys nothing here.
+        # GNU ld accepts and ignores it, which is why this went unnoticed, but
+        # lld -- the default linker of the CLANG64 environment -- refuses it:
+        #     lld: error: unknown argument: -Bsymbolic-functions
+        set(SYMBOLIC_FLAGS "")
     else()
         set(NO_UNDEFINED_FLAGS "-Wl,--no-undefined")
         # -Bsymbolic-functions: replace dynamic symbols used internally in
